## Problem

A karate academy teaching at two locations needed a site that ranked for local
searches, listed class times accurately, and could take an enquiry — and that
the owner could update without me. The usual answers each fail one of those.
A hosted site builder makes the content easy and the SEO and cost opaque. A
CMS-backed dynamic site means a server, a database and a patching schedule for a
site that changes a few times a month.

## Approach

Serve a fully static build from S3 through CloudFront, with no origin server at
all. Put the content in the repository as YAML and Markdoc, and put a CMS in
front of it — Keystatic, which commits directly to GitHub, so an edit is a
commit and a deploy rather than a database write. The only two things that can't
be static — the contact form and Keystatic's OAuth token exchange — are one
small Lambda each. All of it is Terraform; there is no
console click that isn't reproducible.

## Architecture

**Site.** Astro 7 in static output with React 19 islands, Tailwind 4 and
shadcn components. Content lives in `src/content` — locations, testimonials,
FAQs, gallery and a couple of Markdoc pages — and is edited through Keystatic's
admin at `/keystatic`, backed by a GitHub App. Each location emits its own
`SportsActivityLocation` JSON-LD node with address, telephone and opening hours,
addressed by a stable `@id` so other schema blocks can reference that exact
business node rather than creating an anonymous duplicate.

**Delivery.** A private S3 bucket with all public access blocked; its policy
grants `s3:GetObject` only to `cloudfront.amazonaws.com`, conditioned on this
distribution's ARN. CloudFront reaches it through an Origin Access Control
signing SigV4. A CloudFront Function on viewer-request does directory-index
rewriting (`/langley` → `/langley/index.html`), keeps the Keystatic SPA's
sub-routes on its single entry point, and stamps `x-forwarded-host`. A response
headers policy sets HSTS, CSP, frame options and a `Permissions-Policy`.

**Contact.** A Node Lambda behind an HTTP API, throttled at 5 requests/second
with a burst of 10, sending through SES. It validates required fields, length
caps and email shape, and carries a honeypot `company` field — filled by bots,
invisible to users — which returns `200` without sending, so the bot learns
nothing.

**Operations.** An account-wide CloudTrail trail plus CloudFront and S3 access
logs, all into one logs bucket on split retention — access logs expire at 90 days
because they contain visitor IPs, CloudTrail's management-event history is kept
for 365 — and CloudWatch alarms on contact-form flood, API 5xx, Lambda errors and
CloudFront request volume, all into one SNS topic.

**Deploys.** GitHub Actions assumes one of two OIDC roles — `gha-deploy`, which
can only sync the bucket and create an invalidation, and `gha-terraform`, which
manages the infrastructure. No AWS access key exists anywhere.

## Decisions and trade-offs

**Private bucket plus OAC, not a public website bucket.** S3 static website
hosting is one checkbox and gives you directory indexes for free. It also serves
plain HTTP from a bucket-level public grant that anyone can hit directly,
bypassing CloudFront, its cache and its security headers. Going private moves
directory-index resolution into a CloudFront Function — real work, and the reason
the rewrite logic has a `/api/` guard for the Lambda-origin behaviour. In
exchange there is exactly one way to reach an object.

**WAF is provisioned but off by default.** The web ACL exists in
`infra/waf.tf` behind `var.enable_waf`. At roughly $8/month against a static site
that already sits behind CloudFront and Shield Standard, with the only dynamic
route throttled at API Gateway and alarmed in CloudWatch, paying continuously for
managed rule groups is buying insurance against a risk the architecture has
mostly removed. The flag means turning it on during an incident is one apply —
and turning it *off* is documented as a two-step detach, because Terraform races
the delete against the detach and fails.

**Terraform's own role is scoped by workspace, not shared.** Staging and
production share one state bucket, so `gha-terraform`'s state permissions are
computed from `terraform.workspace` — each workspace's applied policy names only
its own state key. Prod state holds the Keystatic GitHub App secret in plaintext;
staging's auto-applying role must not be able to read it.

**The OIDC trust policy was the hard part.** Two things broke it in production.
Setting `environment:` on a job silently rewrites the token's `sub` claim to
`repo:<repo>:environment:<name>`, so every `AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` started
failing with "Not authorized" until the policies matched. And because this repo
was renamed, GitHub issues an *immutable*, ID-suffixed subject —
`igor-siergiej@79415930/taisei-karate@1312344887` — so the plain `owner/repo`
value fails every time. Both are now comments in `infra/` rather than folklore;
the second is [written up in full](/blog/static-sites-without-click-ops/).

## Outcome

Live at [taiseikarate.uk](https://taiseikarate.uk). Content edits go through
Keystatic and deploy themselves; infrastructure changes run `terraform plan` on
the PR and apply on merge. With no origin server, the standing cost is S3,
CloudFront and two Lambdas that only run on a form submission or a CMS login.

The shape is reusable and has been extracted into a scaffold,
[`content-website-template`](https://github.com/igor-siergiej/content-website-template),
so the next content site starts from this rather than from a blank Terraform
directory. The honest caveat is that this repo's own README is still the
template's — it documents the scaffold, not the site that grew out of it.
